IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `theft-nouns`

-- Theft Act 1968, sections 1 to 6: the definition of theft. Layer 1.
--
-- Each rule is quoted from ../../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1968-60.txt line by line (the
-- "-- src:N | ..." lines are checked by srcrefs.py) and cited with @ref.
-- Rule names begin with the section so that no name is defined twice anywhere
-- in the encoding.
--
-- Reach of these definitions. Section 1(3) says ss 2 to 6 "apply only for
-- purposes of this section" unless the Act otherwise provides. So s 2 is NOT the
-- meaning of "dishonestly" in ss 13, 17, 20, 22 and 24A: there the word is a fact
-- the person states (NOTES.md, fork 1). Sections 4(1) and 5(1) are carried across
-- the whole Act by s 34(1). Sections 1(2) and 2(2) are asserted in
-- theft-cases-s01-06.l4, not turned into rules, because they only say that
-- something does not matter.

§ `Sections 1 to 6 - the definition of theft`

§§ `Section 1 - basic definition of theft`

-- src:7 | (1) A person is guilty of theft if he dishonestly appropriates property belonging to another with the intention of permanently depriving the other of it; and “thief” and “steal” shall be construed accordingly.
-- src:8 | (2) It is immaterial whether the appropriation is made with a view to gain, or is made for the thief’s own benefit.
-- src:9 | (3) The five following sections of this Act shall have effect as regards the interpretation and operation of this section (and, except as otherwise provided by this Act, shall apply only for purposes of this section).
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 1 (src:7-9)
GIVEN t IS A `Theft facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 1 guilty of theft` t MEANS
        "dishonestly" ... `s 2 the appropriation is dishonest` (t's `the dishonesty`)
    AND "appropriates" ... `s 3 he appropriates, and the appropriation can amount to theft` (t's `the appropriation`)
    AND "property" ... `s 4 the thing is property that can be stolen` (t's `the thing taken`)
    AND "belonging to another" ... `s 5 the property belongs to another` (t's `the belonging`)
    AND "with the intention of permanently depriving the other of it" ... `s 6 he intends permanently to deprive the other of it` (t's `the intention`) (t's `the belonging`)

§§ `Section 2 - "Dishonestly"`

-- src:11 | (1) A person’s appropriation of property belonging to another is not to be regarded as dishonest—
-- src:12 | (a) if he appropriates the property in the belief that he has in law the right to deprive the other of it, on behalf of himself or of a third person; or
-- src:13 | (b) if he appropriates the property in the belief that he would have the other’s consent if the other knew of the appropriation and the circumstances of it; or
-- src:14 | (c) (except where the property came to him as trustee or personal representative) if he appropriates the property in the belief that the person to whom the property belongs cannot be discovered by taking reasonable steps.
-- src:15 | (2) A person’s appropriation of property belonging to another may be dishonest notwithstanding that he is willing to pay for the property.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 2 (src:11-15)
-- s 2 gives only the cases in which an appropriation is NOT to be regarded as
-- dishonest. It gives no positive test, so outside those cases dishonesty is a fact
-- the person states: `dishonest apart from the section 2(1) beliefs`. Section 2(2)
-- (willingness to pay) therefore protects no one, and `willing to pay for the
-- property` is read by no rule.
GIVEN d IS A `Dishonesty facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 2 the appropriation is dishonest` d MEANS
        NOT (`s 2(1) the appropriation is not to be regarded as dishonest` d)
    AND d's `dishonest apart from the section 2(1) beliefs`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 2(1) (src:11-14)
GIVEN d IS A `Dishonesty facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) the appropriation is not to be regarded as dishonest` d MEANS
        "(a)" ... d's `believed in law he had the right to deprive the other of the property`
    OR  "(b)" ... d's `believed he would have the other's consent if the other knew`
    OR  "(c)" ... (    d's `believed the owner cannot be discovered by taking reasonable steps`
                   AND NOT (d's `the property came to him as trustee or personal representative`))

§§ `Section 3 - "Appropriates"`

-- src:17 | (1) Any assumption by a person of the rights of an owner amounts to an appropriation, and this includes, where he has come by the property (innocently or not) without stealing it, any later assumption of a right to it by keeping or dealing with it as owner.
-- src:18 | (2) Where property or a right or interest in property is or purports to be transferred for value to a person acting in good faith, no later assumption by him of rights which he believed himself to be acquiring shall, by reason of any defect in the transferor’s title, amount to theft of the property.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 3 (src:17-18)
-- s 3(2) bars THEFT by a good-faith purchaser; it does not say he did not
-- "appropriate". So it is a bar on the appropriation limb of s 1(1), here.
GIVEN a IS AN `Appropriation facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 3 he appropriates, and the appropriation can amount to theft` a MEANS
        `s 3(1) he appropriates` a
    AND NOT (`s 3(2) a defect in the transferor's title is no theft` a)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 3(1) (src:17)
GIVEN a IS AN `Appropriation facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) he appropriates` a MEANS
        "any assumption of the rights of an owner" ... a's `assumed the rights of an owner`
    OR  "a later assumption by keeping or dealing with it as owner" ...
            (    a's `came by the property without stealing it`
             AND a's `later assumed a right to it by keeping or dealing with it as owner`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 3(2) (src:18)
GIVEN a IS AN `Appropriation facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 3(2) a defect in the transferor's title is no theft` a MEANS
        a's `property or an interest in it was transferred, or purported to be, to him for value`
    AND a's `acted in good faith when it was transferred to him`
    AND a's `the rights assumed are rights he believed himself to be acquiring`
    AND a's `the only ground for calling it theft is a defect in the transferor's title`

§§ `Section 4 - "Property"`

-- src:20 | (1) “Property” includes money and all other property, real or personal, including things in action and other intangible property.
-- src:21 | (2) A person cannot steal land, or things forming part of land and severed from it by him or by his directions, except in the following cases, that is to say—
-- src:22 | (a) when he is a trustee or personal representative, or is authorised by power of attorney, or as liquidator of a company, or otherwise, to sell or dispose of land belonging to another, and he appropriates the land or anything forming part of it by dealing with it in breach of the confidence reposed in him; or
-- src:23 | (b) when he is not in possession of the land and appropriates anything forming part of the land by severing it or causing it to be severed, or after it has been severed; or
-- src:24 | (c) when, being in possession of the land under a tenancy, he appropriates the whole or part of any fixture or structure let to be used with the land.
-- src:25 | For purposes of this subsection “land” does not include incorporeal hereditaments; “tenancy” means a tenancy for years or any less period and includes an agreement for such a tenancy, but a person who after the end of a tenancy remains in possession as statutory tenant or otherwise is to be treated as having possession under the tenancy, and “let” shall be construed accordingly.
-- src:26 | (3) A person who picks mushrooms growing wild on any land, or who picks flowers, fruit or foliage from a plant growing wild on any land, does not (although not in possession of the land) steal what he picks, unless he does it for reward or for sale or other commercial purpose.
-- src:27 | For purposes of this subsection “mushroom” includes any fungus, and “plant” includes any shrub or tree.
-- src:28 | (4) Wild creatures, tamed or untamed, shall be regarded as property; but a person cannot steal a wild creature not tamed nor ordinarily kept in captivity, or the carcase of any such creature, unless either it has been reduced into possession by or on behalf of another person and possession of it has not since been lost or abandoned, or another person is in course of reducing it into possession.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 4 (src:20-28)
-- s 4(1) is an open list ("includes"), so the person states whether the thing
-- is property at all: `something that is not property within section 4(1)`.
-- In s 4(2) "land" excludes incorporeal hereditaments (line 25); in s 4(3)
-- "mushroom" includes any fungus and "plant" includes any shrub or tree
-- (line 27). Those are the reach of the two `Kind of thing` constructors that
-- carry the words; the person does not state them separately.
GIVEN t IS A `Thing taken`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4 the thing is property that can be stolen` t MEANS
    CONSIDER t's `kind of thing`
        WHEN `money` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a thing in action or other intangible property` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `other personal property` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `land itself` THEN
            `s 4(2)(a) breach of confidence by a person authorised to dispose of land` t
        WHEN `a thing forming part of land` THEN
            `s 4(2) one of the exceptions to the bar on stealing land applies` t
        WHEN `mushrooms, or flowers, fruit or foliage, picked from a plant growing wild on land` THEN
                `s 4(2) one of the exceptions to the bar on stealing land applies` t
            AND `s 4(3) wild produce picked for reward, sale or other commercial purpose` t
        WHEN `a wild creature that is tamed or ordinarily kept in captivity` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `a wild creature that is neither tamed nor ordinarily kept in captivity, or the carcase of one` THEN
            `s 4(4) another has reduced it into possession or is reducing it into possession` t
        WHEN `something that is not property within section 4(1)` THEN FALSE

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 4(2) (src:21-25)
GIVEN t IS A `Thing taken`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(2) one of the exceptions to the bar on stealing land applies` t MEANS
        "(a)" ... `s 4(2)(a) breach of confidence by a person authorised to dispose of land` t
    OR  "(b)" ... `s 4(2)(b) a person not in possession of the land severs something forming part of it` t
    OR  "(c)" ... `s 4(2)(c) a tenant in possession appropriates a fixture or structure let with the land` t

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 4(2)(a) (src:22)
GIVEN t IS A `Thing taken`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(a) breach of confidence by a person authorised to dispose of land` t MEANS
        t's `authorised as trustee, personal representative, attorney or otherwise to sell or dispose of land belonging to another`
    AND t's `dealt with it in breach of the confidence reposed in him`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 4(2)(b) (src:23)
GIVEN t IS A `Thing taken`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(b) a person not in possession of the land severs something forming part of it` t MEANS
        NOT (t's `in possession of the land`)
    AND t's `appropriated it by severing it, causing it to be severed, or after it had been severed`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 4(2)(c) and the tenancy definition (src:24-25)
GIVEN t IS A `Thing taken`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(2)(c) a tenant in possession appropriates a fixture or structure let with the land` t MEANS
        (    t's `in possession of the land under a tenancy`
         OR  t's `remains in possession of the land after a tenancy ended, as statutory tenant or otherwise`)
    AND t's `it is a fixture or structure let to be used with the land`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 4(3) (src:26-27)
GIVEN t IS A `Thing taken`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(3) wild produce picked for reward, sale or other commercial purpose` t MEANS
    t's `picked it for reward, or for sale or other commercial purpose`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 4(4) (src:28)
GIVEN t IS A `Thing taken`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 4(4) another has reduced it into possession or is reducing it into possession` t MEANS
        t's `it has been reduced into possession by or on behalf of another person and possession has not since been lost or abandoned`
    OR  t's `another person is in course of reducing it into possession`

§§ `Section 5 - "Belonging to another"`

-- src:30 | (1) Property shall be regarded as belonging to any person having possession or control of it, or having in it any proprietary right or interest (not being an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest).
-- src:31 | (2) Where property is subject to a trust, the persons to whom it belongs shall be regarded as including any person having a right to enforce the trust, and an intention to defeat the trust shall be regarded accordingly as an intention to deprive of the property any person having that right.
-- src:32 | (3) Where a person receives property from or on account of another, and is under an obligation to the other to retain and deal with that property or its proceeds in a particular way, the property or proceeds shall be regarded (as against him) as belonging to the other.
-- src:33 | (4) Where a person gets property by another’s mistake, and is under an obligation to make restoration (in whole or in part) of the property or its proceeds or of the value thereof, then to the extent of that obligation the property or proceeds shall be regarded (as against him) as belonging to the person entitled to restoration, and an intention not to make restoration shall be regarded accordingly as an intention to deprive that person of the property or proceeds.
-- src:34 | (5) Property of a corporation sole shall be regarded as belonging to the corporation notwithstanding a vacancy in the corporation.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5 (src:30-34)
GIVEN b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5 the property belongs to another` b MEANS
        `s 5(1) another has possession or control of it, or a proprietary right or interest in it` b
    OR  `s 5(2) another has a right to enforce a trust to which the property is subject` b
    OR  `s 5(3) he received it from or on account of another and must retain and deal with it in a particular way` b
    OR  `s 5(4) he got it by another's mistake and must make restoration` b
    OR  `s 5(5) it is property of a corporation sole` b

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5(1) (src:30)
GIVEN b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(1) another has possession or control of it, or a proprietary right or interest in it` b MEANS
        "possession or control" ... b's `another had possession or control of it`
    OR  "any proprietary right or interest" ...
            (    b's `another had a proprietary right or interest in it`
             AND NOT (b's `every such interest of another is an equitable interest arising only from an agreement to transfer or grant an interest`))

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5(2) first limb (src:31)
GIVEN b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(2) another has a right to enforce a trust to which the property is subject` b MEANS
        b's `it was subject to a trust`
    AND b's `another had a right to enforce the trust`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5(3) (src:32)
GIVEN b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(3) he received it from or on account of another and must retain and deal with it in a particular way` b MEANS
        b's `he received it from or on account of another`
    AND b's `he was under an obligation to that other to retain and deal with it or its proceeds in a particular way`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5(4) first limb (src:33)
GIVEN b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(4) he got it by another's mistake and must make restoration` b MEANS
        b's `he got it by another's mistake`
    AND b's `he was under an obligation to make restoration, in whole or in part, of it, its proceeds or its value`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5(5) (src:34)
GIVEN b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(5) it is property of a corporation sole` b MEANS
    b's `it is property of a corporation sole, the office being vacant or not`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5(2) second limb (src:31)
GIVEN i IS AN `Intention facts`
      b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(2) an intention to defeat the trust is an intention to deprive` i b MEANS
        `s 5(2) another has a right to enforce a trust to which the property is subject` b
    AND i's `meant to defeat the trust`

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 5(4) second limb (src:33)
GIVEN i IS AN `Intention facts`
      b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 5(4) an intention not to make restoration is an intention to deprive` i b MEANS
        `s 5(4) he got it by another's mistake and must make restoration` b
    AND i's `meant not to make restoration`

§§ `Section 6 - "With the intention of permanently depriving the other of it"`

-- src:36 | (1) A person appropriating property belonging to another without meaning the other permanently to lose the thing itself is nevertheless to be regarded as having the intention of permanently depriving the other of it if his intention is to treat the thing as his own to dispose of regardless of the other’s rights; and a borrowing or lending of it may amount to so treating it if, but only if, the borrowing or lending is for a period and in circumstances making it equivalent to an outright taking or disposal.
-- src:37 | (2) Without prejudice to the generality of subsection (1) above, where a person, having possession or control (lawfully or not) of property belonging to another, parts with the property under a condition as to its return which he may not be able to perform, this (if done for purposes of his own and without the other’s authority) amounts to treating the property as his own to dispose of regardless of the other’s rights.
@ref Theft Act 1968 s 6 (src:36-37)
GIVEN i IS AN `Intention facts`
      b IS A `Belonging facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6 he intends permanently to deprive the other of it` i b MEANS
        "meaning the other permanently to lose the thing itself" ... i's `meant the other permanently to lose the thing itself`
    OR  `s 6(1) he means to treat the thing as his own to dispose of regardless of the other's rights` i
    OR  `s 6(2) he parts with it under a condition as to return he may not be able to perform` i
    OR  `s 5(2) an intention to defeat the trust is an intention to deprive` i b
    OR  `s 5(4) an intention not to make restoration is an intention to deprive` i b

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 6(1) (src:36)
GIVEN i IS AN `Intention facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(1) he means to treat the thing as his own to dispose of regardless of the other's rights` i MEANS
        i's `meant to treat the thing as his own to dispose of regardless of the other's rights`
    AND (    NOT (i's `the way he meant to treat it was a borrowing or lending`)
         OR  i's `the borrowing or lending was for a period and in circumstances making it equivalent to an outright taking or disposal`)

@ref Theft Act 1968 s 6(2) (src:37)
GIVEN i IS AN `Intention facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 6(2) he parts with it under a condition as to return he may not be able to perform` i MEANS
        i's `had possession or control of the property, lawfully or not`
    AND i's `parted with the property under a condition as to its return which he may not be able to perform`
    AND i's `parted with it for purposes of his own`
    AND i's `parted with it without the other's authority`
